{"parentUuid":"a8e589af-327a-4055-a3ba-8f3b7215da4a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a21d52add52be96a7","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01Kj6CskQ4pDdoXinv4qW888","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011tUQ6Abp1qCc9p3P5RJFFb","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"self-play data generation neural combinatorial optimization larger instances"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1309,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4270,"cache_read_input_tokens":21131,"output_tokens":298,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4270},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1309,"output_tokens":298,"cache_read_input_tokens":21131,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4270,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4270,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaorfjtPurRZe4BMZjnHQ","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"4dbba140-f5b6-41c8-a288-56a311aec43c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:48.950Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"67209b11-1b83-4f4a-a3cc-3e537e38c456","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a21d52add52be96a7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013UpCPqLyVAmx7ED5gPEdjn","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"transfer learning combinatorial optimization pretrained model finetune routing scheduling\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Is Transfer Learning Helpful for Neural Combinatorial Optimization Applied to Vehicle Routing Problems? - IMT Mines Alès\",\"url\":\"https://imt-mines-ales.hal.science/hal-03717063\"},{\"title\":\"Transfer Reinforcement Learning for Combinatorial Optimization Problems | MDPI\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/1999-4893/17/2/87\"},{\"title\":\"Quick-Tune: Quickly Learning Which Pretrained Model to Finetune and How\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2306.03828v4\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - dongjinkun/COPZoo: Neural Combinatorial Optimization · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/dongjinkun/COPZoo\"},{\"title\":\"Is Transfer Learning Helpful for Neural Combinatorial Optimization Applied to Vehicle Routing Problems? | Computing and Informatics\",\"url\":\"https://www.cai.sk/ojs/index.php/cai/article/view/2022_1_172\"},{\"title\":\"Recent Advances in Deep Learning for Routing Problems · The ICLR Blog Track\",\"url\":\"https://iclr-blog-track.github.io/2022/03/25/deep-learning-for-routing-problems/\"},{\"title\":\"An in-depth study to fine-tune the hyperparameters of pre-trained transfer learning models with state-of-the-art optimization methods: Osteoarthritis severity classification with optimized architectures - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2210650224001780\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - Thinklab-SJTU/awesome-ml4co: Awesome machine learning for combinatorial optimization papers. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Thinklab-SJTU/awesome-ml4co\"},{\"title\":\"Neural Combinatorial Optimization for Real-World Routing\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2503.16159v1\"},{\"title\":\"Mastering Model Optimization: In-Depth Guide to Fine-Tuning and Transfer Learning in Machine… | by Jillani Soft Tech | Artificial Intelligence\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/artificial-intelligence-blocbelt/mastering-model-optimization-in-depth-guide-to-fine-tuning-and-transfer-learning-in-machine-bc47ae4def92\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Key Research Areas\n\n**Neural Combinatorial Optimization and Transfer Learning:**\n\nNeural Combinatorial Optimization (NCO) is the study of data-driven solvers for NP-Hard problems based on neural networks, and transfer learning can improve model training by leveraging knowledge learned while solving similar tasks.\n\n**Application to Routing and Scheduling Problems:**\n\nTransfer learning has been studied specifically on routing problems like the Traveling Salesman Problem (TSP) and Vehicle Routing Problem (VRP), where researchers evaluate the effect of applying transfer learning from a model trained on TSP while training a model to solve Capacitated VRP (CVRP).\n\nThe integration of transfer learning with automated reinforcement learning in combinatorial optimization is an emerging research area, with applications to the asymmetric traveling salesman problem (ATSP) and sequential ordering problem (SOP).\n\n**Architecture and Methods:**\n\nGraph Neural Networks are typically the architecture of choice at the core of deep learning-driven solvers for routing problems.\n\nNeural combinatorial optimization models can be broadly classified as autoregressive approaches that build solutions step-by-step or non-autoregressive models that produce solutions in one shot, and can be trained via supervised learning or reinforcement learning.\n\n**Recent Advances:**\n\nThe research landscape includes recent developments in areas like Meta-SAGE (Scale Meta-Learning Scheduled Adaptation with Guided Exploration) for mitigating scale shift on combinatorial optimization and other novel approaches for vehicle routing and scheduling problems.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"6b4ec627-b259-4481-9495-59d11c276a4c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:54.794Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"67209b11-1b83-4f4a-a3cc-3e537e38c456","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"b107c4ad-c159-48b0-80fd-cd8da2d16fb7","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a21d52add52be96a7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CN5xCThFU9P6mqB8A4r8x1","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"VLSI hierarchical placement floorplanning multi-scale optimization algorithm\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CHAPTER 10 Floorplanning Tung-Chieh Chen\",\"url\":\"https://cc.ee.ntu.edu.tw/~ywchang/Courses/PD_Source/EDA_floorplanning.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Floorplanning of VLSI by Mixed-Variable Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2401.15317v1\"},{\"title\":\"A Quasi-Newton-based Floorplanner for fixed-outline floorplanning - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0305054821000174\"},{\"title\":\"Fixed-outline floorplanning: enabling hierarchical design: IEEE Transactions on Very Large Scale Integration (VLSI) Systems: Vol 11, No 6\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1109/TVLSI.2003.817546\"},{\"title\":\"29 An Effective Floorplan-Guided Placement Algorithm for Large-Scale\",\"url\":\"https://home.engineering.iastate.edu/~cnchu/pubs/j53.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Multilevel Optimization for Large-Scale Hierarchical FPGA Placement | Journal of Computer Science and Technology | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11390-010-9389-y\"},{\"title\":\"Floorplanning of VLSI by Mixed-Variable Optimization | SpringerLink\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-981-97-4393-3_12\"},{\"title\":\"An Optimal Partitioning and Floor Planning for VLSI Circuit Design Based on a Hybrid Bio-Inspired Whale Optimization and Adaptive Bird Swarm Optimization (WO-ABSO) Algorithm | Journal of Circuits, Systems and Computers\",\"url\":\"https://www.worldscientific.com/doi/10.1142/S0218126623502730\"},{\"title\":\"A stepwise refinement algorithm for integrated floorplanning, placement and routing of hierarchical designs | IEEE Conference Publication | IEEE Xplore\",\"url\":\"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/521448/\"},{\"title\":\"A PSO-based intelligent decision algorithm for VLSI floorplanning | Soft Computing | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00500-009-0501-6\"}]\n\nI've performed a web search for VLSI hierarchical placement floorplanning multi-scale optimization algorithms. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Overview of VLSI Floorplanning Approaches\n\nFloorplanning is a critical stage in the physical design of VLSI circuits that determines the performance of VLSI chips to a large extent. It is a complex optimization problem with multiple objectives and constraints.\n\n## Main Algorithm Categories\n\nFloorplanning algorithms generally fall into two categories: the floorplanning algorithm based on combinatorial optimization model (FA-COM) and the floorplanning algorithm based on analytic optimization model (FA-AOM).\n\n## Hierarchical Approaches\n\nPATOMA (PArtitioning To Optimize Module Arrangement) is based on a hierarchical paradigm and first uses a top-down recursive bipartition process to partition the original circuit into a set of subcircuits to reduce the global interconnections. Then, it uses rectangle packing algorithms to solve leaf subcircuits to produce a floorplan.\n\n## Multi-Scale/Multilevel Optimization\n\nA multilevel placer for hierarchical designs is based on multilevel optimization method which combines the multilevel bottom-up clustering process and top-down placement process into a V-cycle.\n\n## Recent Mixed-Variable Optimization Methods\n\nRecent approaches formulate floorplanning of VLSI as a mixed-variable optimization problem, addressing discrete orientation variables by distribution evolutionary algorithms and continuous coordination variables by conjugate sub-gradient algorithms.\n\n## Bio-Inspired Algorithms\n\nParticle swarm optimization (PSO) has been applied to obtain feasible floorplanning in VLSI circuit physical placement.\n\nThe search results show that modern VLSI floorplanning uses a combination of hierarchical decomposition, multi-scale optimization, and various meta-heuristic approaches to handle the complexity of large-scale designs.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"9805349e-a889-4d49-8918-bce5f9d8d397","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:55.202Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b107c4ad-c159-48b0-80fd-cd8da2d16fb7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"a8e589af-327a-4055-a3ba-8f3b7215da4a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a21d52add52be96a7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PDwrX4LfHgNw4FhuH9mPaQ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT solution hint warm start initial assignment OR-Tools\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT, UNKNOWN status with complete solution hint · Issue #3750 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3750\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"Coding Patterns - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/06_coding_patterns.html\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"Google AI’s OR-Tools CP-SAT Solver unofficial tutorial Bobak Pezeshki\",\"url\":\"https://ics.uci.edu/~dechter/courses/ics-275/fall-2020/slides/OR-Tools_CP-SAT_Solver_Tutorial.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT/Python how to provide an intial solution · Issue #1152 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/1152\"},{\"title\":\"ortools.sat.python.cp_model API documentation\",\"url\":\"https://or-tools.github.io/docs/pdoc/ortools/sat/python/cp_model.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT/Python how to save an initial solution and use it for next stage optimization · Issue #1542 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/1542\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding the [hint] in the CP-SAT log\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/wsvOc-66-84\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query about CP-SAT solution hints, warm starts, and initial assignments in OR-Tools. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Solution Hints and Warm Starting\n\nIf you have a good intuition about how the solution might look—perhaps from solving a similar model or using a good heuristic—you can inform CP-SAT to incorporate this knowledge into its search. Some workers will try to follow these hints, which can significantly improve the solver's performance if they are good.\n\nIf the hints actually represent a feasible solution, the solver can use them to prune the search space of all branches that have worse bounds than the hints.\n\n## Adding Hints in Python\n\nThe `add_hint()` method adds 'var == value' as a hint to the solver, appending the variable index and value to the model's solution hint.\n\n## Complete vs Partial Hints\n\nCP-SAT only benefits from complete hints (all variables hinted). A function can complete partial hints into a full variable assignment by doing a quick solve with hinted variables fixed, then setting hints for all remaining variables based on the solution.\n\n## Warm Starting Pattern\n\nOne optimization is warm-starting, where the solver uses the previous solution as a starting point or \"hint\" for the next iteration. This technique can significantly speed up the solving process, as the solver can often use the previous solution as a good starting point for repair.\n\nThe search results also reveal some known issues, such as when solver stops by time limit in presolve, CP-SAT can't provide solution from hints, which may be a limitation to be aware of.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"dcb1309a-c598-47a7-a68e-cf656898941e","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:56.239Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a8e589af-327a-4055-a3ba-8f3b7215da4a","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"4dbba140-f5b6-41c8-a288-56a311aec43c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a21d52add52be96a7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011tUQ6Abp1qCc9p3P5RJFFb","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"self-play data generation neural combinatorial optimization larger instances\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Self-Improved Learning for Scalable Neural Combinatorial Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2403.19561v3\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - dongjinkun/COPZoo: Neural Combinatorial Optimization · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/dongjinkun/COPZoo\"},{\"title\":\"Self-Improvement for Neural Combinatorial Optimization: Sample Without Replacement, but Improvement\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2403.15180v2\"},{\"title\":\"[2403.19561] Self-Improved Learning for Scalable Neural Combinatorial Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.19561\"},{\"title\":\"Neural Combinatorial Optimization with Heavy Decoder: Toward Large Scale Generalization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2310.07985v2\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - Thinklab-SJTU/awesome-ml4co: Awesome machine learning for combinatorial optimization papers. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Thinklab-SJTU/awesome-ml4co\"},{\"title\":\"Self-Improvement for Neural Combinatorial Optimization: Sample Without Replacement, but Improvement | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=agT8ojoH0X\"},{\"title\":\"Multi-Action Self-Improvement For Neural Combinatorial Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.12273\"},{\"title\":\"Take a Step and Reconsider: Sequence Decoding for Self-Improved Neural Combinatorial Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2407.17206v1\"},{\"title\":\"Mastering construction heuristics with self-play deep reinforcement learning | Neural Computing and Applications\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1007/s00521-022-07989-6\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found about self-play data generation for neural combinatorial optimization on larger instances:\n\n## Key Findings\n\n**Self-Improved Learning Approaches:**\n\nA self-improved mechanism enables direct model training on large-scale problem instances without labeled data by iteratively generating better solutions as pseudo-labels to guide efficient model training.\n\n**Training Without Expert Data:**\n\nA common approach involves sampling multiple solutions for random instances using the current model in each epoch and then selecting the best solution as an expert trajectory for supervised imitation learning. This allows training larger architectures without expert data through a simple, problem-independent training scheme that decodes solutions for randomly generated problem instances in each epoch using the current model.\n\n**Handling Large-Scale Instances:**\n\nA linear complexity attention mechanism can efficiently handle large-scale combinatorial problem instances with low computation overhead. Additionally, models trained only on small-scale problem instances can achieve robust and promising performance on problems with much larger sizes.\n\n**Multi-Agent Self-Improvement:**\n\nRecent work has explored generating multi-agent actions in parallel to drastically accelerate the solution generation phase of the self-improvement loop, demonstrating consistent improvements in solution quality and reduced generation latency.\n\nThese approaches represent significant advances in making neural combinatorial optimization practical for real-world large-scale problems without requiring expensive expert solutions for training data.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"d39153ce-5088-401e-893d-6022d5be0d8f","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:56.468Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4dbba140-f5b6-41c8-a288-56a311aec43c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
