{"parentUuid":"a698af62-fcf6-41c2-b15d-bf1f84d4dba8","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a21d52add52be96a7","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01V4DMLqxsqX9mHs1rZeVSKq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ND3uBvwf9JPsxZA93BFLMj","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"algorithmic curriculum problem decomposition rule-based small to large constraint programming"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7320,"cache_read_input_tokens":25401,"output_tokens":307,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7320},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":307,"cache_read_input_tokens":25401,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7320,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7320,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaorghAxmptMsWxpZyexF","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"50ad7a84-a689-4a1f-8ce7-57026d7b174f","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:42:01.421Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"ca65770d-a53e-4dac-b9f0-68c2ef990ec1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a21d52add52be96a7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016QPsBnJDnPxtV2Sn7XsaF1","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"distribution shift detection neural combinatorial optimization out-of-distribution generalization\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"ASAP: Exploiting the Satisficing Generalization Edge in Neural Combinatorial Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2501.17377\"},{\"title\":\"Distribution shift, generalization and OOD challenge in offline reinforcement learning: a comprehensive survey | Neural Computing and Applications | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00521-026-11966-8\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - huytransformer/Awesome-Out-Of-Distribution-Detection: Out-of-distribution detection, robustness, and generalization resources. The repository contains a curated list of papers, tutorials, books, videos, articles and open-source libraries etc · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/huytransformer/Awesome-Out-Of-Distribution-Detection\"},{\"title\":\"Enhancing neural combinatorial optimization by progressive training paradigm - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0925231225023793\"},{\"title\":\"Robust Generalization despite Distribution Shift via Minimum Discriminating Information – Optimization Online\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/2021/06/8436/\"},{\"title\":\"[2210.10897] Window-Based Distribution Shift Detection for Deep Neural Networks\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.10897\"},{\"title\":\"Graph OOD Generalization\",\"url\":\"https://graph.ood-generalization.com/\"},{\"title\":\"Distribution Shift Detection for Deep Neural Networks | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=yZCpZrUqzK0\"},{\"title\":\"Probing Neural Combinatorial Optimization Models\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.22131v1\"},{\"title\":\"Control+Shift: Generating Controllable Distribution Shifts | Springer Nature Link (formerly SpringerLink)\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-91907-7_19\"}]\n\nHere are the search results for your query on distribution shift detection, neural combinatorial optimization, and out-of-distribution generalization:\n\n## Key Findings\n\n**Neural Combinatorial Optimization and Distribution Shift:**\n\nA recent framework called ASAP (Adaptive Selection After Proposal) was designed to address the critical challenge of distribution shift in Neural Combinatorial Optimization. By identifying the Satisficing Generalization Edge and decoupling the decision-making process into a robust proposal policy and a lightweight selection policy, this approach effectively balances cross-distribution generalization with rapid online adaptation.\n\nDeep Reinforcement Learning has been applied to Combinatorial Optimization problems like TSP and Vehicle Routing, but neural solvers often exhibit brittleness when facing distribution shifts, with neural policies trained on specific data distributions often performing poorly when tested on new instances with different characteristics.\n\n**Out-of-Distribution Generalization in NCO:**\n\nA progressive training paradigm for Neural Combinatorial Optimization effectively enhances the performance of the model in terms of in-distribution test and out-of-distribution generalization. NCO models with superior generalization performance learn transferable representations, that is, features that generalize beyond the training distribution and enable robust performance on unseen problem instances.\n\n**Distribution Shift Detection:**\n\nDistribution shift detection methods for deep neural networks aim to detect input distributional deviations by continuously monitoring the operation of the network and firing off an alarm whenever a deviation is detected.\n\nThe search results reveal a growing body of research at the intersection of these three areas, with recent work focusing on both detecting distribution shifts and developing robust neural combinatorial solvers that can generalize effectively.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"8aeac5bf-9799-4bcc-9629-abb14b232612","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:42:06.768Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ca65770d-a53e-4dac-b9f0-68c2ef990ec1","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"9e82e643-0b2f-4306-aca4-83a0f547cb27","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a21d52add52be96a7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KvXvVWnETtMphXg8TJsEgk","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"divide and conquer\" CP-SAT large neighborhood search subproblem decomposition placement\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Divide-and-conquer algorithm - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divide-and-conquer_algorithm\"},{\"title\":\"A divide-and-conquer strategy for integer programming problems\",\"url\":\"https://aimspress.com/article/id/685aa0fdba35de6e26810699\"},{\"title\":\"Chapter 5 Cuppen’s Divide and Conquer Algorithm\",\"url\":\"https://people.inf.ethz.ch/arbenz/ewp/Lnotes/chapters5-6.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Divide and conquer algorithms (article)\",\"url\":\"https://www.khanacademy.org/computing/computer-science/algorithms/merge-sort/a/divide-and-conquer-algorithms\"},{\"title\":\"A divide-and-conquer algorithm for distributed optimization on networks - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1063520323001100\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction to Divide and Conquer Algorithm - GeeksforGeeks\",\"url\":\"https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/dsa/introduction-to-divide-and-conquer-algorithm/\"},{\"title\":\"Applications of Divide and Conquer | Yet Another Competitive Programming Blog\",\"url\":\"https://mzhang2021.github.io/cp-blog/divide-and-conquer/\"},{\"title\":\"Divide and Conquer - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/mathematics/divide-and-conquer\"},{\"title\":\"Divide and Conquer Algorithm - GeeksforGeeks\",\"url\":\"https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/dsa/divide-and-conquer/\"},{\"title\":\"Divide-and-Conquer Algorithm - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/divide-and-conquer-algorithm\"}]\n\nI found some relevant results from your search query. Here's a summary of what the search returned:\n\nThe search results include general information about divide-and-conquer algorithms and a particularly relevant paper on applying divide-and-conquer strategies to integer programming problems.\n\nOne approach partitions decision variables based on constraint relationships, optimizes them in smaller subproblems, and progressively merges solutions while repairing constraints. Large neighborhood search (LNS) is an existing heuristic that improves efficiency but lacks a structured decomposition strategy.\n\nA divide-and-conquer strategy for integer programming consists of five main steps: (1) Initialization, obtaining an initial feasible solution; (2) Partitioning, grouping decision variables based on constraints; (3) Directional optimization, solving subproblems independently; (4) Merging, combining solutions while ensuring feasibility; and (5) Refinement, applying a fixed-radius local search.\n\nThis method has been tested on four standard benchmarks—minimum vertex cover (MVC), maximum independent set (MIS), set cover (SC), and combinatorial auction (CA)—and demonstrated significant improvements over both state-of-the-art solvers like Gurobi and SCIP, and advanced LNS-based heuristics.\n\nHowever, the search results did not return specific papers focusing on CP-SAT (Constraint Programming - Satisfiability), placement problems, or the specific combination of these terms with large neighborhood search. If you're looking for more specialized information about CP-SAT implementations with these decomposition strategies, you may want to search with more specific terms or check research papers from constraint programming venues.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"52fae386-692b-4331-88c9-693a3a0a23f7","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:42:07.050Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9e82e643-0b2f-4306-aca4-83a0f547cb27","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"a698af62-fcf6-41c2-b15d-bf1f84d4dba8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a21d52add52be96a7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vc3BMA2Eu3vtfCQdQBMKZz","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"progressive scaling training small to large instances neural solver TSP CVRP\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Select and Optimize: Learning to solve large-scale TSP instances Hanni Cheng\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v206/cheng23a/cheng23a.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Enhancing neural combinatorial optimization by progressive training paradigm - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0925231225023793\"},{\"title\":\"GLOP: Learning Global Partition and Local Construction\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2312.08224\"},{\"title\":\"Neural TSP Solver with Progressive Distillation\",\"url\":\"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/26432/26204\"},{\"title\":\"Neural TSP Solver with Progressive Distillation | Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence\",\"url\":\"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/26432\"},{\"title\":\"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2025\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/file/b210c387381713a14a4f5a607aff3520-Paper-Conference.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Learning to Reduce Search Space for Generalizable Neural Routing Solver\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2503.03137\"},{\"title\":\"Neural Combinatorial Optimization with Heavy Decoder: Toward Large Scale Generalization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2310.07985v2\"},{\"title\":\"Rethink Efficiency Side of Neural Combinatorial Solver: An Offline and Self-Play Paradigm\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.20730v1\"},{\"title\":\"Self-Improved Learning for Scalable Neural Combinatorial Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.19561\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here are the key findings on progressive scaling training for neural solvers on TSP and CVRP problems:\n\n## Progressive Training Methods\n\nA progressive training paradigm with the LEHD model has been evaluated on TSP and CVRP, with extensive experiments indicating that the paradigm significantly improves performance in both in-distribution tests and out-of-distribution generalization.\n\nA novel progressive distillation framework adopts curriculum learning to train TSP samples in increasing order of their problem size and progressively distills high-level knowledge from small models to large models via a distillation loss.\n\n## Specific Implementation Example\n\nIn one progressive distillation approach, when training on TSP100, a pre-trained TSP50 model is applied as the teacher network for knowledge transfer, with the generated distillation loss incorporated into the training objective. For TSP150, TSP100 is selected as the teacher network since the number of cities in the local window is 0.4×150 = 60.\n\n## Generalization Performance\n\nBy training on small-scale problem instances, the LEHD model can generate nearly optimal solutions for TSP and CVRP with up to 1000 nodes and achieves state-of-the-art performance at various scales up to size 1000, while generalizing well to solve real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib problems.\n\nA learning-to-reduce method trained solely on 100-node instances from uniform distribution generalizes remarkably well to large-scale TSP and CVRP instances with up to 1 million nodes from uniform distribution and over 80K nodes from other distributions.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"83333ef3-ed38-4123-aa8e-bc9645fabf26","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:42:08.437Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a698af62-fcf6-41c2-b15d-bf1f84d4dba8","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"50ad7a84-a689-4a1f-8ce7-57026d7b174f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a21d52add52be96a7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ND3uBvwf9JPsxZA93BFLMj","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"algorithmic curriculum problem decomposition rule-based small to large constraint programming\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Constraint Satisfaction Problems (CSP) in Artificial Intelligence - GeeksforGeeks\",\"url\":\"https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/artificial-intelligence/constraint-satisfaction-problems-csp-in-artificial-intelligence/\"},{\"title\":\"Handbook of Constraint Programming Edited by F. Rossi, P. van Beek and T. Walsh\",\"url\":\"https://www.dcs.gla.ac.uk/~pat/cpM/papers/CP_Handbook-20060315-final.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Logic, Optimization and Constraint Programming J. N. Hooker •\",\"url\":\"https://johnhooker.tepper.cmu.edu/joc2.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Constraint programming Alexander Bockmayr John N. Hooker May 2003 Contents 1\",\"url\":\"https://johnhooker.tepper.cmu.edu/cp-hb.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Decomposition-based Synthesis for Applying Divide-and-Conquer-like Algorithmic Paradigms | ACM Transactions on Programming Languages and Systems\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3648440\"},{\"title\":\"Fast and parallel decomposition of constraint satisfaction problems - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9360169/\"},{\"title\":\"Operations Research Methods in Constraint Programming\",\"url\":\"https://johnhooker.tepper.cmu.edu/chapter15.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Constraint Programming\",\"url\":\"https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?repid=rep1&type=pdf&doi=d9f83f0dd71b489a2d35e0c77714cf40fa508f26\"},{\"title\":\"Constraint programming - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constraint_programming\"},{\"title\":\"J. LOGIC PROGRAMMING 1994:19,20:503-581 503 CONSTRAINT LOGIC PROGRAMMING:\",\"url\":\"https://courses.grainger.illinois.edu/cs522/sp2016/ConstraintLogicProgrammingASurvey.pdf\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query about \"algorithmic curriculum problem decomposition rule-based small to large constraint programming.\" The search returned results related to constraint programming, problem decomposition techniques, and algorithmic paradigms. \n\nHere's a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Constraint Programming & Problem Decomposition:**\n\nProblem decomposition is a vital aspect of modeling in constraint satisfaction, where you must decide how to divide the problem and where to make simplifying approximations.\n\n**Rule-Based Approaches:**\n\nIn the rules iteration approach to constraint propagation, reduction rules specify conditions under which domain reductions can be performed for a constraint.\n\n**Decomposition Methods:**\n\nPowerful decomposition methods have been developed to overcome complexity in CSPs, though computing such decompositions for instances poses challenges, with approaches like Generalized Hypertree Decompositions (GHDs) being used.\n\n**Algorithm Application & Decomposition:**\n\nProblem decomposition methods can divide a whole problem into simpler subtasks using component elimination and variable elimination to make subtasks tractable with existing solvers.\n\nThe search results primarily cover general constraint programming methodologies, decomposition techniques, and rule-based constraint propagation. If you're looking for more specific information about \"small to large\" curricula or algorithmic curriculum learning specifically, you may want to refine your search with additional keywords.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"1cf35add-0b89-4838-a330-dfe24634e507","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:42:09.342Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"50ad7a84-a689-4a1f-8ce7-57026d7b174f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"1cf35add-0b89-4838-a330-dfe24634e507","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a21d52add52be96a7","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01RyeD68HvN8g9K1W8x8bdPU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have enough material. Let me grab one more piece on the progressive training paradigm paper and Meta-SAGE for scale shift, then synthesize."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5652,"cache_read_input_tokens":32721,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5652,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId":"req_011CaorheYywQs9qFHRabEKs","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"ac69919b-2fed-477f-9ccb-9e39f33bdc32","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:42:12.401Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
